  "The oversight side acquired a new failure mode. Two rounds of retraining cut a sandbagging audit's AUC from 0.978 to 0.560 while hiding 40-49 accuracy points, and AI Security Institute authors report a \"task badness\" activation direction that rises in the turns before a model reward-hacks — a detector and a way around a detector in the same week.",
  "The responsibility half of the question is still unanswered, and the first mover was a company rather than a regulator. United Airlines banned third-party personal AI agents from booking flights, saying \"sharing account credentials with personal, third-party, AI assistants can expose sensitive information\", and citing federal rules requiring travelers to verify in person that luggage contains no banned substances. In the same days OpenAI and Google connected their own agents to payment cards and bank accounts, and the European Commission's Digital Fairness Act proposal would, on Tech Policy Press's reading of Article 26(9), ban interfaces that require consumers to use automated systems at all. No regulator or court has assigned liability for an agent incident."
