  "In Congress the measures remain plans. Reuters reports the incoming Democratic Congress plans bills on AI testing, liability and data-centre electricity costs, none introduced. Senator Murray pressed OpenAI on ChatGPT advice that tapered a Seattle woman off her anti-seizure medication, which is oversight by letter rather than by statute. The White House AI task force moved in the other direction, urging agencies to limit liability for good-faith voluntary cyber-incident reporting.",
  "The comparison the arc has been drawing is now explicit: a government elsewhere did in one announcement what Congress has not. Australia said on 10 November it will legislate six mandatory obligations on all frontier AI companies wishing to operate there, enforced by a new regulator able to levy significant penalties. Nothing equivalent has been introduced in the United States, and the data-centre measures voters passed on 3 November have not yet been shown to stop a permitted project."
